      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's denote the integer by \\( n \\). According to the problem, when \\( n \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\]\nThis means that \\( n \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe need to find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. First, let's find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3. We substitute \\( n = 15k + 7 \\) into the division by 3:\n\\[ n \\equiv 15k + 7 \\pmod{3} \\]\nSince \\( 15k \\) is a multiple of 3, it leaves a remainder of 0 when divided by 3. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3} \\]\nNow, we need to find the remainder when 7 is divided by 3. We calculate:\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\nThus, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n\nNext, let's find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5. We substitute \\( n = 15k + 7 \\) into the division by 5:\n\\[ n \\equiv 15k + 7 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\( 15k \\) is a multiple of 5, it leaves a remainder of 0 when divided by 5. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{5} \\]\nNow, we need to find the remainder when 7 is divided by 5. We calculate:\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThus, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\nThe sum of the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5 is:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
